According to APLer  <APLer@floor.tilde>:
> You *never* get to choose what you polymorph into.

Unless you wear a ring of polymorph control, in which case you get to
choose what you polymorph into; that's the whole point of that ring,
actually. There is still a 20% chance you polymorph into a "new man"
instead, but otherwise you get the monster you chose, at least if that
is a monster into which polymorphing is allowed; no risk of being turned
into an unwanted form, e.g. a large monster which breaks your cloak.

Without polymorph control, you can still get predictable polymorphs
in some situations:
- if you are a wererat/werejackal/werewolf, then you turn into the
corresponding animal, or back to human if you were in animal form
(not that it is really useful, but that's at least predictable);
- if you wear dragon scales or dragon scale mail, then you turn into
the corresponding dragon -- and the scales/scale mail are not lost in
the process (you get them back when you turn back to human form);
- if you are currently polymorphed into a vampire then you turn into
a vampire bat (which is kinda lousy).

The polymorph-to-dragon is a solution to the problem at hand, since
dragons fly. Remember to remove your cloak, though (the dragon scales or
scale mail are saved, but any worn cloak is destroyed, because dragons
are large animals).


> Polymorph is little more than a fools wish in nethack IMHO. 

Polymorph (made "permanent" with an amulet of unchanging) allows for
extremely powerful characters, especially in the end game. For instance,
a vampire lord has an awesome natural AC, is human-shaped (thus can wear
all armor types), flies, regnerates, has natural slow digestion, does
not breathe, and is totally immune to death attacks, _including_ those
from Death itself. Another good form is an aleax, which gets doubled
attacks (thus four blows per turn if two-weaponing, plus an extra kick
attack).

And, more important than all of the above: self-polymorph is fun !


